<h2>Color Palette: What Works and What Ruins the Look</h2>
<p>The Transitional palette is built on a warm-neutral base — think greige, warm white, soft taupe, oatmeal — because these tones are flexible enough to read as either warm or cool depending on the light, which is exactly the ambiguity this style depends on. Walls are typically kept one to two shades lighter than furniture to let statement pieces stand out without the room feeling flat.</p>
<p>Secondary colours stay muted: sage green, dusty blue, warm grey. Accent colours are used sparingly and with intention — a single deep navy armchair, a terracotta cushion set, brushed brass hardware — rather than scattered across multiple elements. This restraint is what separates Transitional from Eclectic or Bohemian, which intentionally layer many colours at once.</p>
<h3>Common colour mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Going pure white:</strong> Reads as clinical/Modern, not warm/Transitional. Always add warmth — a hint of beige or grey undertone.</li>
  <li><strong>Using more than 2 accent colours:</strong> Immediately tips the room toward Eclectic and breaks the calm.</li>
  <li><strong>Mismatched lighting temperature:</strong> A warm palette under cool-white (5000K+) lighting looks washed out and sterile. Stick to 2700K–3000K for Transitional interiors.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Materials: What Goes Into a Transitional Home</h2>
<p>Material choice is where Transitional design earns its practicality. It leans on materials that look premium but perform well under daily Indian household use — humidity, dust, and heavy foot traffic all considered.</p>
